UNIT parse_type
LANG ts
PRIMARY-SHA256 13ec282f2ac414f28d67d0f388026277b9171b3b207288adedef231a2b912999
SIG export function parse_type(s: string): TypeNode
NOTE TypeNode = { k: 'text' | 'bytes' | 'bool' | 'none' | 'i64' | 'u64' | 'f64' | 'json' } | { k: 'union'; alts: TypeNode[] } |
  { k: 'tuple'; items: TypeNode[] } | { k: 'list'; item: TypeNode } | { k: 'map'; key: TypeNode; val: TypeNode } |
  { k: 'record'; fields: [string, TypeNode][] }, declared and exported by the artifact
ABI TypeScript compiles to the js runtime: text = string; each node a fresh plain object; lists are Arrays; a record field
  is a [string, TypeNode] tuple. STRICT: the artifact type-checks under strict: true with no any, no non-null assertion
  and no ts-ignore. A throw (R11) is a thrown Error; the message is not graded.
ENVELOPE as the primary.
MECHANISM (restated from js) WHITESPACE (R1) is an explicit test against the four WS characters — never
  String.prototype.trim and never the regex class \s. NAME (R7) is the IDENT pin applied at the cursor. Field uniqueness (R9)
  is exact string equality. SAME TYPE (R5) is a structural comparison that ignores union alt order and record field order —
  a set comparison, or an identity string built with the alts' and the fields' strings SORTED (never JSON.stringify of the
  nodes as written, which keeps order). The nesting cap (R10) is a depth counter checked before descending. COVERS (R6) is a recursive
  predicate over two parsed nodes, every clause (a)–(h) tried — it is not a set test on the rendered strings.
DEPENDS-ON none
PINNED regex IDENT = /^[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*/ CITED R7 and R9 — applied to the text from the cursor; no u or i flag
PROVENANCE rederive@bb8ded84fa50 cli/sir/canon.mjs parseType. Language file re-cut 2026-10-27 with the round-5 primary.
